Checks: research/surv_excess_check.py, then the build: observed + correction = spherical, triangle
  closure, log/distance, sine rule; the build fails on a disagreement that is not flagged.
What could still be wrong: A misread digit in an angle that no check covers could pass; flagged rows
  say why they are kept.
Collected: 2026-10-28
Licence: public-domain
Sources:
- C. A. Schott, The Transcontinental Triangulation and the American Arc of the Parallel, U.S. Coast
  and Geodetic Survey Special Publication No. 4 (Washington, 1900): triangles of the base nets and
  adjusted triangles of the main triangulation; resulting geographic positions
  <https://geodesy.noaa.gov/library/pdfs/Special_Publication_No_4.pdf>
- C. A. Schott, The Eastern Oblique Arc of the United States and Osculating Spheroid, U.S. Coast and
  Geodetic Survey Special Publication No. 7 (Washington, 1902): resulting angles and sides; synopsis
  of the base lines (p. 100); resulting geographic positions
  <https://geodesy.noaa.gov/library/pdfs/Special_Publication_No_7.pdf>
- J.-B. Delambre and P. Méchain, Base du système métrique décimal (Paris, 1806-1810): t. 1 pp.
  513-542 (Tableau général des triangles), t. 2 p. 56 (the bases) and pp. 800-841 (Tableau complet
  des triangles), t. 3 Table V (positions); ETH-Bibliothek Zürich copy
  <https://doi.org/10.3931/e-rara-11803>
- T. Maclear, Verification and Extension of La Caille's Arc of Meridian at the Cape of Good Hope
  (London, 1866), pp. 533-558 (principal and secondary triangles) and 589 (positions); Cornell copy
  <https://archive.org/details/cu31924012313437>
- W. Roy, An account of the trigonometrical operation whereby the distance between the meridians of
  the Royal Observatories of Greenwich and Paris has been determined, Phil. Trans. 80 (1790)
  111-270, pp. 64-79 of the offprint (calculation of the triangles)
  <https://archive.org/details/b28763324>
- F. G. W. Struve, Arc du méridien de 25° 20′ entre le Danube et la mer Glaciale, vol. 1 (St
  Petersburg, 1860): p. 76 (the six bases), tableaux A (pp. 96-102), C, D, E (pp. 111-143)
  <https://archive.org/details/dli.pahar.0518>
Evidence class (rule 10): measure
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Observed angle sums that stay at 180° whatever the
  size of the triangle, or an excess that does not grow with the area (a constant offset fitting as
  well as the line through zero).
