Notes: Every row was read off the page images (the PDFs' OCR is unusable) by Sonnet subagents and
  checked by research/surv_excess_check.py and by the build (observed+correction=spherical, closure,
  log/distance, sine rule; positions against the printed sides on Clarke 1866). Areas come from the
  measured bases through the observed angles only; the bases are printed reduced to sea level, the
  one place a radius enters (~1e-6 of the lengths). Delambre, Maclear, Roy and Struve (2026-10-28):
  same rules; a triangle whose only fault is a misprinted side keeps its angles (sides are not
  used); a concluded or misprinted angle can still carry lengths as 180° minus the two others,
  never entering the fit; sides two triangles imply through a shared ray (quadrilaterals, Roy) are
  closed by the law of cosines. Roy's 1790 angles cannot resolve the excess on his small triangles
  (shown, bound only).

